## Resources

### Resource base directory

Quite the compatibility show here (newest to oldest):
 * <base>/<vendor>/<product><version> (Used when -DWINPR_USE_VENDOR_PRODUCT_CONFIG_DIR=ON -DWITH_RESOURCE_VERSIONING=ON)
 * <base>/<vendor>/<product> (Used when -DWINPR_USE_VENDOR_PRODUCT_CONFIG_DIR=ON -DWITH_RESOURCE_VERSIONING=OFF)
 * <base>/<product><version> (Used when -DWINPR_USE_VENDOR_PRODUCT_CONFIG_DIR=OFF -DWITH_RESOURCE_VERSIONING=ON)
 * <base>/<product> (Used when -DWINPR_USE_VENDOR_PRODUCT_CONFIG_DIR=OFF -DWITH_RESOURCE_VERSIONING=OFF)
 * <base>/WinPR (used when -DWINPR_USE_LEGACY_RESOURCE_DIR=ON)

### Timezones

When build with -DWITH_TIMEZONE_FROM_FILE=ON the time zone definitions are loaded from TimeZoneMap.json
usually found in the resources directory WINPR_RESOURCE_ROOT.

### Keyboard VK to Scancode mappings

The file KeyboardMapping.json in the resource directory WINPR_RESOURCE_ROOT overrides build in mappings
if it exists and is valid.

#### Format

* The whole configuration is embedded in a JSON object
* In this top level object there is one named object for each WINPR_KBD_TYPE.
* Each object is of the following format:
   * A JSON array named "multivk" with 128 entries (string representation of the VK_* code or a number)
   * A JSON array named "kbdext" with 128 entries (string representation of the VK_* code or a number)
   * A JSON array named "normal" with 128 entries (string representation of the VK_* code or a number)

```
{
    "WINPR_KBD_TYPE_IBM_PC_XT": {
        "multivk": ["VK_NONE", 12, ...]
        "kbdext": ["VK_NONE", 24, ...]
        "normal": ["VK_NONE", 33, ...]
    }
}
```
